 <p class="formula">inches per foot = slope % &times; 12 &divide; 100</p>
 <p>Example: a 250 ft run of 8" sewer at 0.40% falling from 100.00 drops 1.00 ft, so the far end is 99.00. Every 25 ft station is 0.10 ft lower than the one before it. 0.40% is 0.048 inches per foot.</p>
 <p>The rows go every interval from the start, plus one at the end of the run, the same way the full app lays out a grade sheet. Elevations round to the nearest 0.01 ft, with a half rounding away from zero, and feet-inches round to the nearest 1/16". Stations print the way they read on a cut sheet: 125 ft is 1+25.00.</p>
-<div class="note"><p>Check every number against the plans, the cut sheet and your own instrument before you lay pipe. This page does straight grades only. The <a href="/grade-and-cut-sheet-app/">full app</a> handles hubs, fittings, slope changes and rod readings for a whole run.</p></div>
+<div class="note"><p>Check every number against the plans, the cut sheet and your own instrument before you lay pipe. This page does straight grades only. The <a href="/grade-and-cut-sheet-app/">full app</a> handles hubs, fittings, slope changes and rod readings for a whole run.</p>
+<h2>Slope in percent, inches per foot and fall per 100 feet</h2>
+<p>Plans for sewer and storm drain give slope in percent (or in feet per foot: 0.0040 is 0.40%). Plumbing work talks in inches per foot. They are the same thing written three ways:</p>
+<div class="tbl-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th class="num">Slope</th><th class="num">Inches per foot</th><th class="num">Fall in 100 ft</th><th class="num">Feet-inches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="num">0.20%</td><td class="num">0.024"</td><td class="num">0.20 ft</td><td class="num">0'-2 3/8"</td></tr><tr><td class="num">0.40%</td><td class="num">0.048"</td><td class="num">0.40 ft</td><td class="num">0'-4 13/16"</td></tr><tr><td class="num">0.50%</td><td class="num">0.060"</td><td class="num">0.50 ft</td><td class="num">0'-6"</td></tr><tr><td class="num">1.00%</td><td class="num">0.120"</td><td class="num">1.00 ft</td><td class="num">1'-0"</td></tr><tr><td class="num">1.04% (1/8" per foot)</td><td class="num">0.125"</td><td class="num">1.04 ft</td><td class="num">1'-0 1/2"</td></tr><tr><td class="num">2.00%</td><td class="num">0.240"</td><td class="num">2.00 ft</td><td class="num">2'-0"</td></tr><tr><td class="num">2.08% (1/4" per foot)</td><td class="num">0.250"</td><td class="num">2.08 ft</td><td class="num">2'-1"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+<p>To go the other way, percent = inches per foot &divide; 12 &times; 100. A quarter inch per foot is 0.25 &divide; 12 &times; 100 = 2.08%. The minimum slope for a line comes from the plans and the agency's standards, not from this table.</p>
+</div>
